{"qid":"Q902","names":["People's Republic of Bangladesh"],"title":"People's Republic of Bangladesh","endonym":{"native":"বাংলাদেশ","lang":"bn"},"capsule":"Bangladesh declared independence from Pakistan on 26 March 1971, and after a nine-month liberation war in which Indian military intervention proved decisive, Pakistani forces surrendered on 16 December 1971. Sheikh Mujibur Rahman, leader of the Awami League, served as the founding head of government before his assassination in a military coup on 15 August 1975; subsequent decades alternated between military rule and elected governments led by the Awami League and Bangladesh Nationalist Party. The country emerged as a major global garment exporter by the 1990s, and by the 2010s ranked among the world's fastest-growing economies despite recurring political instability and vulnerability to cyclones and flooding.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Bangladesh's borders are precisely established by international agreement and surveyed boundaries; a small land-boundary demarcation with India was completed in 2015 following the exchange of enclaves.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Bangladesh","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bangladesh"},{"label":"Britannica: Bangladesh","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Bangladesh"},{"label":"Willem van Schendel, A History of Bangladesh (Cambridge University Press, 2009)","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/history-of-bangladesh/3A6B7DBF7C1D68EF60D99A9E4C4D3A6E"},{"label":"Rounaq Jahan, Bangladesh: Promise and Performance (University Press Ltd, 2000)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bangla